VALUES (
  'what-is-executive-presence',
  'What Is Executive Presence?',
  'Executive presence is the signals you send in the first eight seconds that make people decide whether to listen, follow or forget you.',
  'Executive presence is the combination of voice, posture and message clarity that makes others trust and follow you before you have finished your first sentence.',
  E'## The real definition of executive presence\n\nExecutive presence is not a title, a suit or a speaking trick. It is the immediate, often unconscious signal that you are worth listening to. Research into first impressions shows that people form a judgement within the first eight seconds of hearing you. In that window they decide if you are credible, composed and relevant.\n\nThe three pillars are clarity, calm and authority. Clarity means your point arrives fast. Calm means your voice and pace do not spike under pressure. Authority means you sound as if you belong in the conversation, not as if you are asking permission to be there.\n\n## Why most professionals misunderstand it\n\nMany people think executive presence is about being louder, more polished or more extroverted. That is a mistake. The most commanding voices in a room are often the quietest. What sets them apart is control: they do not rush, they do not apologise for their point, and they finish their sentences.\n\nIf you are waiting until you have a senior title to develop presence, you are doing it backwards. Presence is what earns the title, not the other way around.\n\n## How Bramwell measures it\n\nBramwell scores your voice against the same four dimensions senior executives are measured on: Structure, Specificity, Confidence Signals and Relevance. You speak for five minutes and receive a Readiness Score out of 100, benchmarked against a CEO target of 88.\n\nThe diagnostic does not flatter you. It shows you exactly where your voice is leaking authority and what to fix first.',
  'Executive presence',
  'what is executive presence',
  '[
    {"question": "Is executive presence the same as charisma?", "answer": "No. Charisma is magnetic personality. Executive presence is controlled signal. You can have presence without being charismatic, and charisma without presence often reads as performative."},
    {"question": "Can executive presence be learned?", "answer": "Yes. It is a set of observable behaviours: pace, pitch control, sentence structure and response patterns. These can be trained and measured."},
    {"question": "How long does it take to improve?", "answer": "Most people notice a shift within two weeks of daily practice. Structural change, where presence becomes automatic, typically takes 30 to 60 days."},
    {"question": "Does executive presence matter in video calls?", "answer": "More than ever. The same vocal signals apply, and because the body is less visible, voice carries even more weight."}
  ]'::jsonb,
  true,
  now()
),
(
  'how-to-develop-executive-presence',
  'How to Develop Executive Presence',
  'Develop executive presence by training four behaviours: structured answers, vocal calm, specific language and confident endings.',
  'You develop executive presence by practising four behaviours: open with a clear point, slow your pace under pressure, replace vague words with specifics, and end sentences without trailing off or apologising.',
  E'## Start with the answer, not the story\n\nThe fastest way to sound more executive is to front-load your point. Most people bury their conclusion inside context, background and qualifiers. The listener has to dig for it.\n\nA simple formula is: point, proof, payoff. State your answer in one sentence, give one concrete example, then say why it matters. This respects the listener''s time and signals that you know your own mind.\n\n## Slow down when it matters\n\nNervous speakers speed up. Executive speakers slow down at the moments that carry weight. If you deliberately pause before an important point, people lean in. If you race through it, they tune out.\n\nRecord yourself answering one question. Count your words per minute. If you are above 160 in a high-stakes moment, you are likely losing authority. Aim for 130 to 150 in the moments that matter.\n\n## Remove the leakage language\n\nWords like "just", "kind of", "maybe" and "I think" leak authority. They tell the listener you do not fully believe what you are saying. Replace "I think we should" with "We should". Replace "This is just my opinion" with silence.\n\nAnother common leak is the upward inflection at the end of statements. It turns every sentence into a question. Record yourself and listen for it.\n\n## Practise with feedback that does not lie\n\nFriends and colleagues will tell you that you sound fine. Bramwell will tell you where you actually lost the room. The five-minute diagnostic scores your voice against a CEO benchmark and gives you the three biggest gaps to fix.',
  'Executive presence',
  'how to develop executive presence',
  '[
    {"question": "What is the fastest way to sound more executive?", "answer": "Front-load your point. Say the answer first, then support it. This one change changes how people perceive your authority."},
    {"question": "Should I speak louder to have more presence?", "answer": "Volume helps only if your voice is currently too soft. More important is pace, pitch control and the absence of filler words."},
    {"question": "How do I stop saying um?", "answer": "Filler words usually fill a silence you are uncomfortable with. Practise pausing instead. A two-second pause sounds thoughtful, not lost."},
    {"question": "Can introverts have executive presence?", "answer": "Yes. Presence is not about being the loudest voice. It is about being the most controlled voice. Introverts often have a natural advantage in calm and brevity."}
  ]'::jsonb,
  true,
  now()
),
(
  'executive-voice-coaching',
  'Executive Voice Coaching: What to Expect',
  'Executive voice coaching trains the sound and structure of how you speak so you are heard, trusted and followed in high-stakes moments.',
  'Executive voice coaching trains the sound and structure of your speaking so you are heard, trusted and followed in meetings, presentations and interviews.',
  E'## What executive voice coaching actually does\n\nTraditional presentation coaching often focuses on slides and body language. Executive voice coaching goes deeper. It trains the vocal and verbal patterns that signal leadership: how you open, how you pace yourself, how you handle pressure and how you close.\n\nThe goal is not to make you sound like someone else. It is to make you sound like the most authoritative version of yourself.\n\n## The four dimensions most coaches ignore\n\nMost coaching is subjective. The best programmes measure four things: Structure, Specificity, Confidence Signals and Relevance. Structure is whether your answer has a clear arc. Specificity is whether you use concrete evidence. Confidence Signals are vocal cues like pace control and absence of filler. Relevance is whether you answer the question that was actually asked.\n\nWhen these are scored, improvement stops being a matter of opinion and starts being data.\n\n## Why AI coaching changes the game\n\nA human coach sees you once a week and remembers what they wrote down. An AI voice coach listens to every practice session, scores every answer and spots patterns a human would miss. You get more reps, more feedback and faster improvement.\n\nBramwell is built as a live AI voice coach. You speak, it scores, it tells you exactly what to fix, and you practise again.\n\n## Is it worth the investment?\n\nIf your income or influence depends on being persuasive in rooms you do not control, voice coaching is one of the highest-leverage investments you can make. The cost of a single missed promotion, lost sale or failed pitch far exceeds the cost of training.\n\nStart with a free five-minute diagnostic to see where you stand against a CEO benchmark of 88.',
  'Voice coaching',
  'executive voice coaching',
  '[
    {"question": "How is voice coaching different from speech therapy?", "answer": "Speech therapy treats clinical issues with voice production. Voice coaching trains communication impact: clarity, authority and persuasion."},
    {"question": "Do I need any equipment?", "answer": "No. You can practise on any device with a microphone. For best results, use headphones and a quiet room."},
    {"question": "How often should I practise?", "answer": "Daily short sessions beat weekly long ones. Fifteen minutes a day for 30 days creates lasting change."},
    {"question": "Will AI coaching replace human coaches?", "answer": "AI replaces the repetitive feedback and scoring. Humans still add judgement, context and high-stakes rehearsal. The best results come from both."}
  ]'::jsonb,
  true,
  now()
)
